// Type checking for the two preload bridges, the last JavaScript the app ships outside the window.
//
// Nothing is compiled and nothing is emitted: this reads preload.js and preload-uninstall.js with
// the JSDoc they carry and says where the code and its own documentation disagree. It used to read
// main.js and every module in src/ the same way, which is how it found a require that had never
// resolved and two handlers that answered every call with "Cannot find module". Since 2026-09-30
// the main process is TypeScript and src/tsconfig.json checks it, strict.
//
// The preloads stay JavaScript on purpose: Electron runs them in the sandbox, as CommonJS, before
// the page, and a preload that failed to load would leave the window with no window.api at all.
//
// Why these settings:
//   checkJs with strict off - the point is to catch what is wrong, not to fail on every value that
//     could be null in two files that only pass messages through.
//   module preserve - each file is read the way it is written.
//   skipLibCheck - Electron's own .d.ts is not this project's to fix.
//
// Not covered: renderer/ and tools/. The renderer is ES modules against the DOM, the tools are a
// mix of both module systems, and each needs its own settings.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "allowJs": true,
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "target": "ES2022",
    "module": "preserve",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": false,
    "types": ["node"]
  },
  "include": ["preload.js", "preload-uninstall.js"]
}
